37,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[7340,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[7343,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[7346,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[7349,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[7853,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[7859,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[7883,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[7892,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[8618,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[8621,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[8624,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[8627,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[9214,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[9247,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[9250,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[10156,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[10324,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[11308,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[15550,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[15553,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[15559,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[15565,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[16444,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[16447,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[16450,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[16453,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[16456,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[19009,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]"],[19228,"[[deeplearning_mit.epub]]\n\n[[featureengineeringformachinelearninganddataanalytics.pdf]]"],[20965,"[[Dialog Management Systems]] decide what should happen next. The dialog manager combines the interpreted input with prior turns, personal or display context, external service state, and a policy. It may ask for a missing value, resolve an elliptical expression, clarify competing interpretations, invoke a service, or recover from a timeout or error.\n\nThis layer turns independent utterances into a conversation. A phrase such as “the second one” can only be understood by retaining the alternatives presented earlier. A correction or help request may interrupt the expected flow and then return to it. Finite-state structures make narrow tasks predictable, while more flexible policies support information arriving in an unexpected order. In either case, the system should preserve what it has understood and avoid making the user restart unnecessarily."],[21440,"[[Digital Information and Early Data Processing]] begins before the electronic computer. Counting boards and mechanical calculators progressively mechanized arithmetic, especially the difficult carry from one decimal position to the next. Punched-card systems then separated information from the machinery that processed it. Herman Hollerith encoded census records as holes, and installations combined key punches, sorters, tabulators, and calculators into flexible data-processing workflows. People supplied the program by carrying decks, setting switches, and deciding which machine should act next.\n\nPunched cards also carried control. Jacquard looms selected threads from encoded patterns, and Charles Babbage planned to use cards to direct a general-purpose Analytical Engine. The same physical medium could therefore represent data or determine action. Telegraphy added long-distance coded communication: Morse pulses anticipated digital signaling, while later information theory formalized the relationship between symbols, codes, and reliable transmission.\n\nThe digital paradigm unified these streams by representing calculation, logic, control, and communication through discrete states. Analog devices modeled quantities with continuous physical relationships, while digital systems manipulated codes. The boundary was initially fluid, but binary electronics made representations easy to copy, store, and transform reliably. A computer became a convergence of calculation, storage, control, input-output, and communication rather than an isolated arithmetic mechanism."],[279,"[[digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf]]"],[1476,"[[digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf]]"],[1485,"[[digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf]]"],[1632,"[[digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf]]"],[2304,"[[digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf]]"],[2610,"[[digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf]]"],[2616,"[[digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf]]"],[2829,"[[digitalvisualeffectsandcompositing.pdf]]"]